 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Photometric Quality vs. Geometry:** High PSNR/SSIM does not guarantee correct geometry; models may render plausible images while misinterpreting structure (e.g., misinterpreting a cabinet as a wall pattern) [S1].
-*   **Uncertainty Handling:** Unlike Focal Loss which up-weights uncertain examples, this method down-weights them for depth supervision, relying more on photometric loss when the depth prior is unreliable [S1].
-*   **Prior Noise:** Using multiple depth hypotheses (as in SCADE) can introduce noise and training instability dependent on random seeds; this method uses a single prior to avoid this [S1].
-*   **Diffusion Uncertainty:** The uncertainty measure is specific to diffusion-based models like DiffDP; it may not be directly applicable to other monocular depth estimators without modification [S1].
+*   **Photometric-Geometric Mismatch:** High photometric quality (PSNR/SSIM) does not guarantee correct geometry. Models may render plausible textures while misinterpreting structure (e.g., misinterpreting a cabinet as a patterned carpet) [S1].
+*   **Uncertainty Handling:** Unlike Focal Loss which emphasizes hard/uncertain examples, this method *downweights* uncertain pixels in the depth loss to prevent the NeRF from learning incorrect geometry from unreliable priors [S1].
+*   **Prior Noise:** Using multiple depth hypotheses (as in SCADE) can introduce noise and training instability. This method uses a single prior to avoid this [S1].
+*   **Diffusion Uncertainty:** The uncertainty measure is specific to diffusion-based models like DiffDP. It may not be directly applicable to other MDE architectures without modification [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time is comparable to standard NeRF training, with additional overhead from the diffusion model inference (pre-computed) and Sinkhorn divergence approximation. The Sinkhorn divergence is approximated using the `geomloss` library with standard hyper-parameters [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the depth prior and uncertainty map for each input image. The NeRF memory footprint is standard.
-*   **Measured Performance:** On ScanNet+, the method reduces baseline RMSE by at least 56% while maintaining comparable photometric results (PSNR/SSIM) [S1]. With DiffDP prior, it reduces initial prior RMSE by 31.4%; with DepthAnything prior, by 18.8% [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** Effective for sparse view settings ($N < 20$). Performance on uncertain pixels (uncertainty > 0.8) improves RMSE by 5.1% when using uncertainty weighting, compared to 1.9% for certain pixels [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Robust to prior inaccuracies; avoids enforcing incorrect geometry; leverages intrinsic uncertainty from diffusion models.
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the availability of a diffusion-based depth prior for uncertainty estimation; Sinkhorn divergence approximation may introduce numerical instability if not tuned [S1].
+
+### Time and Memory Complexity
+*   **Time:** The method adds overhead for the uncertainty calculation (mirrored image inference) and the EMD approximation (Sinkhorn Divergence). The Sinkhorn divergence is an iterative approximation, adding computational cost per step compared to simple L2 depth loss [S1].
+*   **Memory:** Requires storing the depth prior and uncertainty map for each input image. The NeRF model size is standard. The additional 128 samples for EMD increase memory usage slightly during the forward pass [S1].
+
+### Measured Performance
+*   **ScanNet+:** Reduces baseline RMSE by at least 56% while maintaining comparable photometric results (PSNR/SSIM) [S1].
+*   **Prior Reduction:** With DiffDP prior, the EMD objective reduces the initial prior RMSE by 31.4%. With DepthAnything prior, it reduces it by 18.8% [S1].
+*   **Uncertainty Impact:** For pixels with uncertainty $> 0.8$, incorporating $u$ improves RMSE by 5.1%. For pixels with uncertainty $< 0.1$, it helps by only 1.9% [S1].
+
+### Strengths and Limitations
+*   **Strengths:** Robust to noisy depth priors; does not enforce incorrect geometry; leverages intrinsic uncertainty from diffusion models; drop-in replacement for existing depth-supervised NeRFs [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Uncertainty measure is tied to diffusion-based priors (DiffDP); hyperparameters for uncertainty construction can be sensitive; EMD approximation adds computational cost [S1].
+
+### When to Use
+*   Use for sparse-view NeRF reconstruction (e.g., $N < 20$) where geometric accuracy is critical.
+*   Use when off-the-shelf diffusion-based depth priors (like DiffDP) are available.
+*   Use when standard L2 depth supervision leads to geometric inaccuracies due to prior errors [S1].
+
+### When Not to Use
+*   Do not use if only non-diffusion MDE networks are available, as the specific uncertainty measure relies on the denoising process [S1].
+*   Do not use if computational resources are extremely limited, due to the overhead of Sinkhorn Divergence and mirrored image inference [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Rau et al. in a paper titled "Depth-Guided NeRF Training via Earth Mover’s Distance" [S1]. It addresses the limitation of standard NeRFs in sparse view settings where geometry is underconstrained. Previous methods like DäRF supervised rendered depth directly, and SCADE used multiple hypotheses with space-carving loss. This work leverages the uncertainty inherent in diffusion-based depth estimation (DiffDP) and uses EMD to align the ray termination distribution with the prior, providing a more robust supervision signal [S1].
+
+### Origin
+The method was introduced by A. Rau et al. in the paper "Depth-Guided NeRF Training via Earth Mover’s Distance" [S1]. It addresses the issue of NeRFs learning incorrect geometry despite good photometric reconstruction, particularly in sparse-view settings with untextured regions [S1].
+
+### History and Development
+Previous methods like DäRF supervised NeRF-rendered depth directly using L2 loss, which forced the NeRF to approximate potentially inaccurate priors [S1]. SCADE used multiple depth hypotheses but suffered from instability and noise [S1]. This method leverages the uncertainty inherent in diffusion-based depth estimation (DiffDP) to guide the *distribution* of ray termination distances using EMD, providing a more robust supervision signal [S1].
+
+### Usage
+It is used as a drop-in replacement for existing depth-supervised NeRFs, particularly on challenging indoor scenes like ScanNet and ScanNet+ [S1].
 
